.TH REPASS 1 "2026-10-06" "repass" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
repass \- generate passwords and manage a local password vault
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B repass generate
.RB ( \-\-length " N | " \-\-min-length " MIN " \-\-max-length " MAX )"
.BI \-\-separator-kind " KIND"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B repass vault
.RI [ \-\-data-dir " DIR] " command
.br
.B repass record
.RI [ \-\-data-dir " DIR] " command " [options]"
.br
.B repass tag
.RI [ \-\-data-dir " DIR] " command " [options]"
.br
.B repass interactive
.RI [ \-\-data-dir " DIR] [\-\-warnings | \-\-no-warnings]"
.br
.B repass completions
.I SHELL
.SH DESCRIPTION
repass generates passwords without opening a vault and stores records in a
local vault. A record has a name, optional username, host, notes and tags,
and any number of passwords, SSH key pairs, TOTP configurations and codes.
Records, tags and data elements use stable IDs. A data ID identifies an
element within one record; deleted data IDs are not reused.
.PP
Running repass without a command displays help.
Use
.B \-h
for a compact summary and
.B \-\-help
for detailed command help and examples.
This is a personal, educational project.
.SH VAULT DIRECTORY AND PASSWORD INPUT
.B \-\-data-dir DIR
is available on vault, record, tag and interactive commands. Directory
selection uses the explicit option, then
.BR REPASS_DATA_DIR ,
then
.BR $HOME/.repass .
A leading tilde path component expands to HOME; an empty selected directory
is rejected. Generation and completion do not require a data directory.
.PP
Storage commands request the master password through hidden terminal input.
When no vault exists, ordinary storage commands, including
.BR "vault info" ,
create one after requesting a nonempty password and confirmation.
Existing partial vaults or surviving backups are not silently overwritten.
.SS Vault commands
.TP
.B vault init
Create a new vault, refusing existing vault files or backups.
.TP
.B vault info
Show the directory, record and tag counts, tag-catalog status and any failure
to save repaired metadata counts.
.TP
.B vault change-password
Request and confirm a nonempty new master password. Update key metadata
and its backup without rewriting records or tags.
.TP
.B vault recover
Restore missing or damaged files from authenticated, validated .bak snapshots.
Preserve damaged originals as .damaged-* files. Record and tag backups contain
the previous snapshot, so the latest mutation may be lost.
Recovery cannot reconstruct records without a usable backup.
.TP
.B vault finish-init
Complete an interrupted empty-vault initialization. Create empty records only
when authenticated metadata has valid zero counts and no record/tag files or
their backups exist. This does not restore lost records.
.SH PASSWORD GENERATION
Specify an exact
.B \-\-length N
or both inclusive bounds
.B \-\-min-length MIN \-\-max-length MAX
and a separator strategy. Length counts Unicode scalar values, including
separators, rather than bytes or grapheme clusters.
Output contains one password per line. An unreachable target length is an
error; range targets are sampled from the whole range.
.TP
.B \-\-separator-kind KIND
Accept a strategy name or its number:
.RS
.TP
.B 1, none
Join dictionary entries without separators.
.TP
.B 2, between-parts
Insert separators between dictionary entries.
.TP
.B 3, fixed-interval
Insert separators after intervals of content Unicode scalars, independent
of entry boundaries.
.TP
.B 4, fixed-count
Distribute a requested number of insertions across content. Short content
may reduce that number.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-count N
Number of passwords to generate; default: 1.
.TP
.B \-\-dictionary FILE
Use a UTF-8 dictionary with one entry per line, removing line endings.
Entries may contain multiple characters. Conflicts with
.BR \-\-preset .
.TP
.B \-\-preset SET...
Combine built-in character sets: digits, lowercase, uppercase, punctuation,
symbols, brackets, quotes, hash-dollar-percent-caret and backslash-pipe-tilde.
Repeat the option or give several names. Sets form a union; each password
need not contain characters from every selected set.
Without a dictionary or preset, all built-in sets are used.
.TP
.B \-\-shape-selection first|random
Use the first feasible layout (default), or sample uniformly among feasible
layouts. The latter is not uniform sampling among all possible passwords.
.TP
.B \-\-separator TEXT
Nonempty separator text; default: a hyphen. Ignored by none.
.TP
.B \-\-separator-interval N
Positive content interval for fixed-interval; default: 5.
.TP
.B \-\-separator-count N
Positive insertion count for fixed-count; default: 3.
.TP
.B \-\-warnings, \-\-no-warnings
Enable or disable warnings for unused separator options. Warnings are enabled
by default. Unused values are still validated. One-shot generation writes
warnings to stderr and passwords to stdout.
.PP
Limits: 1,000,000 scalars per password, 100,000 passwords per batch and
16,000,000 collected batch scalars, calculated using the maximum target length.
.SH RECORD COMMANDS
.TP
.B record create NAME [options]
Create a record with a nonempty name. Optional metadata:
.BR "\-\-username TEXT" ,
.BR "\-\-host HOST" ,
.BR "\-\-notes TEXT" ,
and
.BR "\-\-tag TAG_ID..." .
Optionally supply one data source; otherwise the record starts with no data.
.TP
.B record list [filters]
List summaries ordered by record ID, without secret values.
.TP
.B record find [\-\-query TEXT] [filters]
Search metadata with a case-insensitive Unicode fuzzy pattern. Match name,
username, host, notes and tag names separately, ranking each record by its
best field score. Order by descending score, then ID.
Secret data values are never searched.
Without a query, use exact filters and order by ID.
.TP
.B record show RECORD_ID [\-\-reveal]
Show metadata and data IDs. Mask data values unless explicitly revealed.
TOTP algorithm, digits and period remain visible.
Creation and update times use YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM (UTC).
.TP
.B record update RECORD_ID [options]
Change only supplied fields. Accept
.BR \-\-name ,
.BR \-\-username ,
.BR \-\-host ,
.BR \-\-notes ,
.BR "\-\-add-tag TAG_ID..." ,
and
.BR "\-\-remove-tag TAG_ID..." .
Remove optional fields with
.BR \-\-remove-username ,
.BR \-\-remove-host ,
or
.BR \-\-remove-notes .
Each field-removal flag conflicts with setting that field.
A data source without \-\-replace-data always adds a new element, including
when passwords already exist. Fields, tags and data can be changed together;
all changes are validated before saving one record update.
.TP
.B record remove RECORD_ID
Remove the record and all its data elements.
.TP
.B record update RECORD_ID DATA_SOURCE
Append one typed data element.
.TP
.B record update RECORD_ID \-\-replace-data DATA_ID DATA_SOURCE
Replace the entire element, retaining its ID. Its type may change.
For SSH, supply both key parts again to retain both: supplying only a public
key removes the previous private part.
.TP
.B record update RECORD_ID \-\-remove-data DATA_ID...
Remove elements; their IDs will not be reused. The same element cannot be
replaced and removed in one operation.
.SS Exact and fuzzy filters
Both list and find accept
.BR "\-\-name NAME" ,
.BR "\-\-host HOST" ,
and
.BR "\-\-tag TAG_ID..." .
Names match in full, case-sensitively. All requested tags must match.
Hosts are IP addresses or domains without protocol, port or path.
IPs compare by parsed value; domains ignore case and a trailing dot.
No DNS lookup is performed.
.PP
Exact filters apply before fuzzy search. Query whitespace is trimmed and
blank queries are rejected. A query is one pattern, with no operator syntax.
Noncontiguous characters support abbreviations. Queries of 1\(en3 Unicode
scalars allow no typos, 4\(en7 allow one, and longer queries allow up to two.
.SH DATA SOURCES
Choose one type per operation. Several elements of the same type may coexist
in a record. In one-shot mode, the master password is requested separately
through the terminal, not read from the data-source stdin stream.
.TP
.B \-\-password-stdin
Read one password line from stdin, removing the final LF or CRLF.
.TP
.B \-\-code-stdin
Read one nonblank recovery code or other code line, removing its line ending.
.TP
.B \-\-totp-stdin
Read one uppercase RFC 4648 Base32 secret line. Accept unpadded encoding or
canonical padding. Store configuration only; no one-time codes are calculated.
Related options require this flag:
.BR "\-\-algorithm sha1|sha256|sha512" " (default: sha1),"
.BR "\-\-digits 6|7|8" " (default: 6), and"
.BR "\-\-period SECONDS" " (positive; default: 30)."
.TP
.B \-\-private-key-file FILE, \-\-private-key-stdin
Read the private SSH key from a UTF-8 file or stdin until EOF.
These alternatives conflict.
.TP
.B \-\-public-key-file FILE, \-\-public-key-stdin
Read the public SSH key from a UTF-8 file or stdin until EOF.
These alternatives conflict.
.PP
SSH requires at least one part; supplied parts cannot be blank. Whitespace
and line endings are preserved, and cryptographic key structure is not parsed.
In one-shot mode only one part can use stdin; use a file for the other part.
.SH TAG COMMANDS
.TP
.B tag create NAME
Create a tag with a unique, nonempty name.
.TP
.B tag list
List tag IDs and names, including technical names for missing catalog entries.
.TP
.B tag rename TAG_ID \-\-name NAME
Set a unique, nonempty name without changing the tag ID.
.TP
.B tag remove TAG_ID
Remove an unused tag. Tags referenced by records cannot be removed;
detach them with record update first.
.TP
.B tag recover
Rebuild a missing or damaged tag-name catalog using currently known tags and
technical names such as #tag-42. This does not recover lost original names.
Use vault recover for restoration from usable backups.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
.B repass interactive
starts a persistent session. Enter commands without the repass prefix;
quotes preserve spaces. Missing required arguments are prompted, but optional
arguments are not. Generation and help do not open the selected vault.
.PP
Passwords, codes and TOTP secrets use hidden terminal input when their
source flags are supplied. SSH stdin flags use ordinary multiline input:
finish each part with a line containing only a period (.). The terminator
is not stored; EOF before it cancels the operation. Both parts can be
entered separately in this mode.
.TP
.B help [COMMAND...], h [COMMAND...]
Show help; for example, help record update.
Required argument markers remain visible even though omitted values are prompted.
.TP
.B vault switch DIR
Close the current vault and select another directory. Open it lazily on the
next storage operation. Ordinary session commands do not accept \-\-data-dir.
.TP
.B warnings [on|off]
Show or change the session warning setting. Defaults to enabled; entry options
\-\-warnings and \-\-no-warnings set it initially. Generation warning flags
override it only for that invocation.
.TP
.B quit, q
Leave the session. EOF also exits; exit is not a supported command.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
.B repass completions SHELL
prints a static script for bash, zsh, fish, powershell or elvish without
opening a vault. Load it in the external shell or save it in that shell's
completion directory. This does not provide Tab completion inside a repass
session and does not modify shell configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate three 16-scalar passwords from digits and lowercase characters:
.PP
.nf
repass generate \-\-length 16 \-\-separator-kind none \e
    \-\-preset digits lowercase \-\-count 3
.fi
.PP
Generate with separators included in the target length:
.PP
.nf
repass generate \-\-length 16 \-\-separator-kind 2 \-\-separator '::'
repass generate \-\-length 20 \-\-separator-kind 3 \e
    \-\-separator-interval 4
repass generate \-\-min-length 12 \-\-max-length 20 \e
    \-\-separator-kind none
.fi
.PP
Use a word dictionary (the target must be reachable):
.PP
.nf
repass generate \-\-length 18 \-\-separator-kind between-parts \e
    \-\-dictionary words.txt \-\-shape-selection random
.fi
.PP
Initialize a separate vault and inspect it:
.PP
.nf
repass vault \-\-data-dir ~/vault init
repass vault \-\-data-dir ~/vault info
.fi
.PP
Store an SSH pair from files and find the record:
.PP
.nf
repass record create server \-\-username alice \e
    \-\-host example.test \-\-private-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 \e
    \-\-public-key-file ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
repass record find \-\-host EXAMPLE.TEST.
repass record find \-\-query alce
.fi
.PP
Read the IDs from list, find, show or tag list before using the examples
below; the numbers are illustrative:
.PP
.nf
repass tag create work
repass tag list
repass record update 1 \-\-add-tag 2
repass record find \-\-query alice \-\-host example.test \-\-tag 2
repass record show 1
repass record show 1 \-\-reveal
repass record update 1 \-\-replace-data 1 \-\-private-key-file new_key \e
    \-\-public-key-file new_key.pub
repass record update 1 \-\-remove-notes \-\-remove-tag 2
repass record update 1 \-\-remove-data 3
.fi
.PP
Enter passwords, TOTP configuration and codes with hidden input in a session.
After each source command, enter the requested value at its hidden prompt:
.PP
.nf
repass interactive
record create mail \-\-username alice \-\-password-stdin
record list
record update 1 \-\-totp-stdin \-\-algorithm sha256 \-\-digits 8
record update 1 \-\-code-stdin
help record update
vault switch ~/other-vault
warnings off
quit
.fi
.PP
One-shot data sources can instead read a redirected file; the master password
is still entered separately through the terminal:
.PP
.nf
repass record update 1 \-\-totp-stdin < totp-secret.txt
.fi
.PP
Change the master password or explicitly restore a backup:
.PP
.nf
repass vault change-password
repass vault recover
.fi
.PP
Load completion in the current Bash shell:
.PP
.nf
source <(repass completions bash)
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B REPASS_DATA_DIR
Default vault directory when \-\-data-dir is absent.
.TP
.B HOME
Used for the default directory and leading-tilde expansion.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set to 1 to disable terminal colors. Redirected output is plain text;
generated passwords and completion scripts have no added color codes.
.SH FILES
Paths below are relative to the selected vault directory.
.TP
.B metadata.repass
Key metadata and authenticated auxiliary counts.
.TP
.B records.repass
Encrypted records and record-ID allocation state.
.TP
.B tags.repass
Optional encrypted tag-name catalog and tag-ID allocation state.
.TP
.B metadata.repass.bak, records.repass.bak, tags.repass.bak
Recovery snapshots. The metadata backup is refreshed on password changes;
record and tag backups retain the previous snapshot.
.TP
.B *.damaged-*
Damaged originals preserved by explicit recovery.
.PP
On Unix, newly created directories use at most 0700 and vault files use at
most 0600. Atomic replacement preserves stricter owner permissions.
Opening an existing vault does not automatically change its permissions.
.SH NOTES
Vault sessions use an exclusive advisory directory lock. A second session
cannot open the same vault until the first closes it.
File replacement is atomic, but updates involving several files are not a
single transaction. Counts are reconciled against readable data on open.
.PP
A missing or unreadable tag catalog does not prevent access to records;
unnamed referenced tags appear as technical names. Ordinary tag operations
do not overwrite a damaged catalog.
.PP
Only current storage formats are accepted. Older formats, including
password-only record schema 1 and legacy vault.repass files, are not migrated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR bash (1)
